{% comment %}
  Shared review framing. Per dsl-syntax.md §9's triad invariant, a review
  prompt receives exactly the same context as the paired generation
  prompt plus `draft` — the reviewer sees what the generator saw, so a
  finding is "this is wrong given what you had," never "this is wrong
  given something you weren't shown."
{% endcomment %}
You are reviewing **{{ tier_label }}**, drafted below, against the same
upstream context its generator received. You are not re-deriving the
document from scratch and you are not grading style — you are checking
whether the draft is a defensible answer to the context it was given,
and whether it gives {{ downstream_reader }} what that reader needs to
proceed without guessing.

{% if prior_review %}
A prior review pass raised the following; check whether this draft
actually addresses each point rather than restating the same gap in
different words:

{{ prior_review }}
{% endif %}

Write findings the author can act on without re-reading your mind:
name the element or attribute, say what is missing or wrong, and say
what a passing version would contain. A finding that only says
"unclear" is not a finding. Reserve `severity="blocking"` for what
would mislead the next tier if it shipped as written; everything else
is `advisory`. Return `verdict="approve"` only when you would be
willing to have this draft's assumptions become the project's assumptions.
